\paragraph{Architectural implications.}
Attention ablation at L1 confirms functional specialization: removing instruction-dominant heads' attention to instruction tokens increases action $R^2$ by $+0.059$ ($p < 0.0001$), while removing non-dominant heads yields only $+0.017$.
The effect is confined to L1 ($|\Delta R^2| < 0.02$ at deeper layers), confirming genuine head specialization without creating a deep-layer bottleneck.
